1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:07,853
The brush uncovers a small stupa in stone: a dome, a railing round it, a mast and tiered umbrellas on top.

2
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:18,331
A stupa begins as something very simple — a mound raised over relics — and becomes the central monument of Buddhist architecture.

3
00:00:18,331 --> 00:00:21,476
Follow the stupa and you can follow the religion.

4
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:29,636
Four sources here, which is as good as this series gets.

5
00:00:29,636 --> 00:00:30,331
The canon.

6
00:00:30,331 --> 00:00:35,008
Inscriptions, including donor records carved on the monuments themselves.

7
00:00:35,008 --> 00:00:37,409
Excavation at monasteries and stupas.

8
00:00:37,409 --> 00:00:42,955
And Chinese travellers, who came looking for texts and wrote down what they found.

9
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:48,465
Four councils are traditionally counted.

10
00:00:48,465 --> 00:00:53,783
The first, at Rajagriha, soon after his death, to agree what he had actually taught.

11
00:00:53,783 --> 00:01:00,965
The second, at Vaishali, about a century later, over points of monastic discipline — and it ended in a split.

12
00:01:03,267 --> 00:01:05,884
The third, at Pataliputra, under Ashoka.

13
00:01:05,884 --> 00:01:12,057
And the fourth, in Kashmir, under Kanishka, where the texts were set down and commented on.

14
00:01:23,700 --> 00:01:26,417
Out of those disagreements come two broad streams.

15
00:01:26,417 --> 00:01:30,892
The older is called Hinayana by the other side, and Theravada by its own adherents,

16
00:01:30,892 --> 00:01:36,092
who hold to the earlier texts in Pali and treat the Buddha as a teacher who showed the way.

17
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:43,311
The later is Mahayana, which uses Sanskrit,

18
00:01:43,311 --> 00:01:47,819
develops the ideal of the bodhisattva — one who delays their own release t

19
00:01:47,819 --> 00:01:52,328
o help others — and treats the Buddha as something greater than a teacher.

20
00:01:52,328 --> 00:01:55,573
A third stream, Vajrayana, develops later still.

21
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:02,662
Now the part that explains the spread.

22
00:02:02,662 --> 00:02:06,775
Buddhism travelled along trade routes, and it travelled with merchants.

23
00:02:06,775 --> 00:02:09,518
Monasteries sat on the roads, offering shelter,

24
00:02:09,518 --> 00:02:14,903
and merchants funded them — the donor inscriptions on the railings say so, name by name.

25
00:02:18,767 --> 00:02:26,780
The religion reached Sri Lanka, Central Asia, China, Southeast Asia and beyond, mostly by the same roads that carried goods.

26
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:31,140
And it built.

27
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:35,068
Stupas, enlarged and encased again and again by later patrons.

28
00:02:35,068 --> 00:02:40,855
Chaitya halls, cut into living rock, with the great horseshoe window over the entrance.

29
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:48,934
And viharas, the monastic dwellings alongside them.

30
00:02:48,934 --> 00:02:56,169
Sanchi, Bharhut, Amaravati, Ajanta, Karle — these are the places where the religion is still standing.

31
00:02:58,467 --> 00:02:59,991
A caution about the councils.

32
00:02:59,991 --> 00:03:05,023
The accounts we have were written down long afterwards, by traditions with a stake in the outcome,

33
00:03:05,023 --> 00:03:09,744
and different schools give different versions of who was present and what was decided.

34
00:03:14,567 --> 00:03:17,785
Some historians treat the sequence as broadly reliable;

35
00:03:17,785 --> 00:03:24,682
others argue the later councils especially are tidied-up retellings that read later divisions back into earlier events.

36
00:03:24,682 --> 00:03:28,086
Learn the four for the exam; hold the detail loosely.

37
00:03:33,433 --> 00:03:36,638
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

38
00:03:36,638 --> 00:03:37,810
Four councils.

39
00:03:37,810 --> 00:03:39,204
Two vehicles.

40
00:03:44,133 --> 00:03:44,890
Dig Quiz.

41
00:03:44,890 --> 00:03:46,328
Pause and name it.

42
00:03:46,328 --> 00:03:52,912
First: a dome raised over relics, with a railing around it and tiered umbrellas above.

43
00:03:52,912 --> 00:03:53,893
A stupa.

44
00:03:56,667 --> 00:04:02,131
Second: a hall cut into living rock, with a great horseshoe window over the entrance.

45
00:04:02,131 --> 00:04:02,829
A chaitya.

46
00:04:02,829 --> 00:04:06,497
Third: the monastic dwelling built or cut beside it.

47
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:11,334
A vihara.

48
00:04:11,334 --> 00:04:16,773
In Mains, a recurring question asks why Buddhism spread beyond India.

49
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:23,898
Trade routes, merchant patronage, royal support, monasteries as infrastructure,

50
00:04:23,898 --> 00:04:28,048
and a teaching that travelled without requiring birth into anything.

51
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,335
A stupa takes its place in the gallery: a mound that became an architecture.

52
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:52,988
Next gallery: the other great tradition of this age, and the one that asked more of its followers.

53
00:04:52,988 --> 00:04:56,832
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

